.Im MicroEmacs command|insert
.ds C0 "(\fB\*cq\fR)\ \&
.ds C1 "(\fB\*e\*cq\fR)\ \&
.so me.tni
.TH quote-char 2
.XI quote-char - "\*(C0Insert literal character"
.SH NAME
quote-char \- Insert literal character
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.I n
.B quote-char
"\fIkey\fR" \*(C0
.br
.I -n
.B quote-char
"\fIcode\fR" \*(C1
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B quote-char
inserts the next typed  character  \fIn\fR  times,  default is 1, ignoring the
fact that it may be a command  character.  \fBquote-char\fR  obeys the current
buffer setting of
.Ht over 2m
mode.
.PP
When \fIn\fR is negative the character is given by its code, \fBquote-char\fR prompts for the
code and inserts the character -\fIn\fR times, e.g. an argument of \fC-1000\fR and the code
\fC75\fR inserts 1000 '\fCu\fR's. The \fIcode\fR is one of:
.IP "\fIhhhh\fR or \fCU+\fIhhhh\fR"
The Unicode character U+\fIhhhh\fR, 1 to 6 hexadecimal digits. A version of \*(mS without
Unicode support inserts the character of the current code page (see
.Ht set-char-mask 2 ),
nothing is inserted if the code page does not have it.
.IP "\fCx\fIhh\fR"
The character \fIhh\fR, 1 or 2 hexadecimal digits, of the current code page, so with a
Western European code page \fCxe9\fR is an 'e' acute. Nothing is inserted if the code page does
not have a character for the byte.
.IP "\fCr\fIhh\fR"
A raw byte, the byte \fIhh\fR is kept and written back to the file as it is whatever the
buffer's
.Ht $buffer-encoding 5 ,
it is displayed as \fC\\x\fIhh\fR. A byte below 0x80 is its \s-1ASCII\s0 character. In a
version of \*(mS without Unicode support it is the same as \fCx\fIhh\fR.
.PP
Only keys which keep the code valid are accepted, the reason a key is rejected being shown after the code, e.g.
"\fC[not hex]\fR" or "\fC[too large, max FF]\fR". The backspace key deletes the last one and a yank adds a copied
code, e.g. "\fCU+20AC\fR". \fBreturn\fR inserts the character, \fBesc\fR or an empty code inserts nothing, and an
abort (\fCC-g\fR) aborts the command. From a macro the \fIcode\fR is the next argument.
.PP
\fBquote-char\fR is bound to \fB\*cq\fR and, with an argument of \fC-1\fR, to \fB\*e\*cq\fR. Both keys may also be used
while entering a search string with
.Ht isearch-forward 2
and at a prompt, \fB\*cq\fR quoting the next key and \fB\*e\*cq\fR (or any key bound to \fBquote-char\fR with a
negative argument) giving the character by its code. An abort while giving the code aborts the search or prompt too,
while \fBesc\fR or an empty code returns to it. A version of \*(mS without Unicode support cannot hold a NUL character,
so a code of 0 is refused.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
.Ht insert-string 2 ,
.Ht isearch-forward 2 ,
.Ht insert-symbol 3 .
.ad
.rm C0
.rm C1
.FH
